WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.308 --> 00:00:01.841
- Comedy and media matters.

00:00:01.841 --> 00:00:02.949
And there's no way that you can say

00:00:02.949 --> 00:00:04.036
that it doesn't matter.

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:04.962
And it affects things.

00:00:04.962 --> 00:00:06.454
So I know that it has an impact,

00:00:06.454 --> 00:00:07.664
but at the same time,

00:00:07.664 --> 00:00:10.090
just because you can find a person

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.515
who is truly and genuinely
offended by what you say,

00:00:13.515 --> 00:00:15.128
does that make it wrong?

00:00:15.128 --> 00:00:15.961
You know what I mean?

00:00:15.961 --> 00:00:16.919
'Cause then at what point
can you say anything?

00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:20.165
(upbeat music)

00:00:20.165 --> 00:00:23.462
- Hey all, welcome to another
episode of Is This Okay?

00:00:23.462 --> 00:00:25.627
Where we talk about things
and ask the question,

00:00:25.627 --> 00:00:27.564
is this really okay?

00:00:27.564 --> 00:00:29.345
Today we are discussing when is something

00:00:29.345 --> 00:00:31.731
considered offensive versus funny,

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.998
and how do you personally draw that line.

00:00:33.998 --> 00:00:36.831
- I do believe that PC is
killing comedy in a way,

00:00:36.831 --> 00:00:38.082
because I feel like jokes should be,

00:00:38.082 --> 00:00:40.012
they should kind of
strike you and be funny.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.454
- They invite conflict 100 percent.

00:00:43.454 --> 00:00:46.157
That's just the personality
that goes into it.

00:00:46.157 --> 00:00:49.631
And they just write jokes
to get people to react.

00:00:49.631 --> 00:00:52.455
- So my whole thing about like, being PC,

00:00:52.455 --> 00:00:54.577
and like oh, you need to not offend people

00:00:54.577 --> 00:00:56.762
and you need to try your
best not hurt people.

00:00:56.762 --> 00:00:59.463
I don't think that's too
much to ask of comedians.

00:00:59.463 --> 00:01:01.569
- As a comedic person, you
realize there's a lot of grey.

00:01:01.569 --> 00:01:03.305
You might get to a
point where you're like,

00:01:03.305 --> 00:01:05.003
you know what, I'm always
gonna offend somebody.

00:01:05.003 --> 00:01:07.531
And then because of that,
you then just go, fuck it.

00:01:07.531 --> 00:01:10.903
And I do think that there
is still something honorable

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:14.233
about at least trying to navigate it.

00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:16.230
You try and be sensitive,
but you also know

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:17.471
you can't please everybody.

00:01:17.471 --> 00:01:18.821
But wait, what was the one you had?

00:01:18.821 --> 00:01:20.356
What was the example
that you had thought of?

00:01:20.356 --> 00:01:23.839
- So, yeah, this is where I draw my line.

00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:27.222
Amy Poehler is the executive
producer of Difficult People.

00:01:27.222 --> 00:01:28.199
- I love that show.

00:01:28.199 --> 00:01:30.433
- Yeah, I don't watch it, but.

00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:33.319
There was a joke on the show.

00:01:33.319 --> 00:01:35.249
Julie, the main character,
tweeted something

00:01:35.249 --> 00:01:37.635
along the lines of like, I can't wait

00:01:37.635 --> 00:01:40.093
for R. Kelly to piss on Blue Ivy.

00:01:40.093 --> 00:01:41.303
Like, until Blue Ivy is older enough

00:01:41.303 --> 00:01:42.574
so R. Kelly can piss on her.

00:01:42.574 --> 00:01:45.895
And, she was kind of like,
you know I did this mean tweet

00:01:45.895 --> 00:01:48.760
but I don't understand why
everyone's so mad at me?

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.472
- Yeah, the context of the...

00:01:50.472 --> 00:01:51.305
(laughter)

00:01:51.305 --> 00:01:52.871
- I love that you're
laughing about it right now.

00:01:52.871 --> 00:01:54.761
- It's like you though
it was funny! Garrick!

00:01:54.761 --> 00:01:56.265
- The context of the joke is that,

00:01:56.265 --> 00:01:59.383
is that, not, you're
looking at the wrong person.

00:01:59.383 --> 00:02:00.665
I think you're looking at Blue Ivy,

00:02:00.665 --> 00:02:02.858
and you're like, oh I hear this
word, I think of an infant.

00:02:02.858 --> 00:02:06.099
You should be looking at R.
Kelly who peed on a person.

00:02:06.099 --> 00:02:08.000
- Well yeah, I'm looking at it both sides.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.788
Yeah, it's like exploitation.

00:02:09.788 --> 00:02:11.268
- It's exploitation of...

00:02:11.268 --> 00:02:14.162
It's R. Kelly who is, I mean,

00:02:14.162 --> 00:02:18.031
arguably a bad dude that
pisses on minors, and-

00:02:18.031 --> 00:02:19.302
- Yeah, I'd argue that.
(laughter)

00:02:19.302 --> 00:02:20.993
- But yeah he does.

00:02:20.993 --> 00:02:24.309
- He's a bad dude, he made a bad call.

00:02:24.309 --> 00:02:25.921
- And I think if I was Beyonce,
and that was my daughter,

00:02:25.921 --> 00:02:27.343
they made a joke about
peeing on my daughter,

00:02:27.343 --> 00:02:28.319
I'd be hella pissed.

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:30.291
- I was offended because I love Beyonce.

00:02:30.291 --> 00:02:32.070
- I love Beyonce!
- And I love Blue.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.021
- I think a lot of really great comics,

00:02:34.021 --> 00:02:38.186
the way that they subvert
PC culture is making it

00:02:38.186 --> 00:02:40.746
that much clearer that this is my opinion,

00:02:40.746 --> 00:02:42.899
and I do not take myself seriously,

00:02:42.899 --> 00:02:45.094
none of them takes themselves seriously.

00:02:45.094 --> 00:02:46.098
- Don't you think it's tough though?

00:02:46.098 --> 00:02:48.211
Because I have a hard time
listening to a comedian

00:02:48.211 --> 00:02:51.430
and he makes a really smart,
witty, social commentary

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.408
comment and I can't lie that
so much of why I like it

00:02:54.408 --> 00:02:56.228
is because I'm like "God,
that's such a good point."

00:02:56.228 --> 00:02:58.909
So I can't give you that
love, then the next thing

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:00.758
you say be "Oh but
nothing he says matters".

00:03:00.758 --> 00:03:03.075
- You're a problematic
person in your everyday life,

00:03:03.075 --> 00:03:05.998
then I don't know if I can
really support you as a comedian.

00:03:05.998 --> 00:03:08.305
- I love offensive jokes,
I watch Margaret Cho

00:03:08.305 --> 00:03:09.576
and I watch Kathy Griffin,

00:03:09.576 --> 00:03:10.843
they both have really offensive jokes,

00:03:10.843 --> 00:03:13.335
but they're done in a way
that is a lot more smart

00:03:13.335 --> 00:03:16.369
and it actually says
something about our society.

00:03:16.369 --> 00:03:17.770
- I think if you're going
to make fun of something

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.832
that is sensitive, it
should be really witty,

00:03:20.832 --> 00:03:22.312
and be like "That's kind of offensive,

00:03:22.312 --> 00:03:24.782
but it was so creative and so well worded.

00:03:24.782 --> 00:03:26.440
That was funny."

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.196
- So is it basically for you, if it's,

00:03:28.196 --> 00:03:30.349
as long as it's funny it's not offensive?

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:32.584
- No, cause there are
some things that, like,

00:03:32.584 --> 00:03:34.749
Eddie Murphy, comic, comedic icon,

00:03:34.749 --> 00:03:38.468
opens up his acts bashing
gay people, essentially,

00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:41.280
and I'm like woah, like
for me watching it,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.303
it was extremely hurtful.

00:03:43.303 --> 00:03:46.208
- It feels harsh, I'm like
eh, maybe he shouldn't be,

00:03:46.208 --> 00:03:49.012
we wouldn't be saying
these things to this day.

00:03:49.012 --> 00:03:50.401
- And it depends on who's
saying the joke too.

00:03:50.401 --> 00:03:52.667
Like a Latino person talking
about the Latino experience

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:55.697
and kind of making fun of our
culture and making it funny,

00:03:55.697 --> 00:03:57.282
you're like that's funny, that's cool.

00:03:57.282 --> 00:03:59.058
- You just have to, I
mean if it's coming from,

00:03:59.058 --> 00:04:00.715
even if it was coming from, like,

00:04:00.715 --> 00:04:02.431
if I heard a black joke from a white guy.

00:04:02.431 --> 00:04:05.104
- That would make you
more mad, it'd have to.

00:04:05.104 --> 00:04:08.294
- It'd kind of, but if he...
(laughter)

00:04:08.294 --> 00:04:10.749
Come on, if he presents himself-

00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:12.671
- If I got on a mic and
started talking about

00:04:12.671 --> 00:04:15.480
the funny things about black
people, you wouldn't be like

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.222
"Wow this is the exact
same reaction I would have

00:04:17.222 --> 00:04:18.563
if a black man were up there."

00:04:18.563 --> 00:04:19.396
- No it's not the same.

00:04:19.396 --> 00:04:20.237
- Yeah, he would not!

00:04:20.237 --> 00:04:21.834
- I'd take my earrings off, like.

00:04:21.834 --> 00:04:23.955
- All the minorities would
be like "Whatchu sayin'?"

00:04:23.955 --> 00:04:25.903
- All four of you would kill me.

00:04:25.903 --> 00:04:28.181
- Right, you know you need to, like,

00:04:28.181 --> 00:04:30.386
sit the fuck down, or whatever.

00:04:30.386 --> 00:04:33.912
But at the same time,
if you come at a place

00:04:33.912 --> 00:04:38.066
where it is like, I feel
like you are with me

00:04:38.066 --> 00:04:39.693
instead of against me.

00:04:39.693 --> 00:04:42.366
- Ally Wang and Bill Burr
are like two of my favorites.

00:04:42.366 --> 00:04:45.129
I know for example when Bill
Burr says something funny,

00:04:45.129 --> 00:04:46.784
how I can listen to
him say the same jokes,

00:04:46.784 --> 00:04:49.640
and one night I'm like "I
didn't feel like you enough

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.842
hatred coming behind that"
and other times I'm like

00:04:51.842 --> 00:04:53.712
"I feel like there was a
lot of hatred behind that."

00:04:53.712 --> 00:04:55.836
- I just hope that I don't
feel like I wasted my money.

00:04:55.836 --> 00:04:58.112
I just want to laugh,
I just want to laugh!

00:04:58.112 --> 00:05:00.417
- People are learning from
each other every day, right?

00:05:00.417 --> 00:05:02.846
And it's a dialogue that
we're just have to be open

00:05:02.846 --> 00:05:03.970
with each other, just learn.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.868
- I understand that it's
grey, but I always lean on

00:05:06.868 --> 00:05:09.246
can I feel what your real intent is.

00:05:09.246 --> 00:05:12.160
If I can feel your real
intent is not malicious,

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.410
it matters a lot to me.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.734
- To be fair though, in certain times,

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:18.101
intent does not matter
if it hurts somebody,

00:05:18.101 --> 00:05:20.124
because people's hurt feelings are valid.

00:05:20.124 --> 00:05:21.088
- How about you?

00:05:21.088 --> 00:05:25.621
- A joke is a joke is a joke.
(laughter)

00:05:25.621 --> 00:05:28.240
Just write good ones, you know?

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.665
A lot of the times, the better the joke,

00:05:31.665 --> 00:05:33.717
the more you'll get away with.

00:05:33.717 --> 00:05:35.301
I'm just a big fan of jokes.

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:38.347
- Okay guys, so when does
comedy become too offensive?

00:05:38.347 --> 00:05:42.414
Leave your comments below,
and we'll see you next week.

00:05:42.414 --> 00:05:44.471
- Your answer sounded so
much cooler than mine.

00:05:44.471 --> 00:05:45.496
(laughter)

00:05:45.496 --> 00:05:48.944
- All, like, poetic, "a joke is a joke".

00:05:48.944 --> 00:05:51.194
(laughter)

